---
/**
 * AI prompt footprint calculator
 *
 * Readers either pick a usage preset or build their own from rows of
 * (model x output length x count per day), which sum into one daily and annual
 * carbon and water cost. That cost is set against a personal footprint they
 * configure (where they live, diet, flying) — with AI carbon estimated on their
 * region's grid — shown as a share of their day, and placed on a daily graph of
 * everyday things and an annual graph of bigger lifestyle choices. State is
 * saved to the URL so an estimate can be shared.
 *
 * Per-model energy / carbon / water estimates (including uncertainty ranges)
 * are generated directly from the EcoLogits library (v0.10), the engine behind
 * the EcoLogits calculator at
 * huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator. All other figures
 * are documented in the methodology section at the bottom of the page.
 */
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const pageTitle = 'What does your AI use cost?';
const pageDescription =
  'Build up how many prompts you send each day to GPT, Claude, and Gemini — at any output length — and see the combined carbon and water cost, set against your own footprint and against everyday things and lifestyle changes.';
const pageUrl = 'https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: pageTitle,
    description: pageDescription,
    url: pageUrl,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com' },
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
    about: [
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Artificial intelligence' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Carbon footprint' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Water footprint' },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Visuals', item: 'https://andymasley.com/visuals' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: pageTitle, item: pageUrl },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  canonicalUrl={pageUrl}
  ogTitle={pageTitle}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <header class="aipf-header">
    <h1 class="aipf-header-title">What does your AI use cost?</h1>
    <p class="aipf-header-intro">
      Pick a usage profile or build your own — each model, at whatever length you typically ask
      for, however many times. The panel adds it all into a single carbon and water cost, sets it
      against your own footprint, and shows how it stacks up against everyday things and the bigger
      choices in your life. Per-model numbers come from
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>.
      Methodology is at the bottom.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="aipf" id="aipf">
    <div class="aipf-presets" id="aipf-presets" aria-label="Usage presets"></div>

    <div class="aipf-grid">
      <!-- LEFT: usage builder -->
      <section class="aipf-models" aria-label="Your daily AI usage">
        <p class="aipf-eyebrow">Your AI usage <span class="aipf-eyebrow-note">— per day</span></p>

        <div class="aipf-rows-head" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>Model</span>
          <span>Typical output</span>
          <span>Per day</span>
          <span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="aipf-rows" id="aipf-rows"></div>
        <button type="button" class="aipf-addrow" id="aipf-addrow">+ Add a kind of use</button>

        <div class="aipf-running" id="aipf-running" aria-live="polite"></div>

        <div class="aipf-toolbar">
          <button type="button" class="aipf-btn" id="aipf-reset">Reset</button>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-btn" id="aipf-share">Copy link to this estimate</button>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- RIGHT: comparison to your own footprint -->
      <section class="aipf-compare" aria-label="How it compares to your footprint">
        <div class="aipf-sticky">
          <div class="aipf-metric-toggle" role="tablist" aria-label="Footprint type">
            <button type="button" class="aipf-metric is-active" data-metric="carbon" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Carbon</button>
            <button type="button" class="aipf-metric" data-metric="water" role="tab" aria-selected="false">Water</button>
          </div>

          <div class="aipf-section aipf-life">
            <p class="aipf-eyebrow">An average day of your life</p>
            <div class="aipf-life-row">
              <label for="aipf-loc">Where you live</label>
              <select id="aipf-loc"></select>
            </div>
            <div class="aipf-life-row">
              <label for="aipf-diet">Your diet</label>
              <select id="aipf-diet"></select>
            </div>
            <div class="aipf-life-row">
              <label for="aipf-fly">How much you fly</label>
              <select id="aipf-fly"></select>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="aipf-result" id="aipf-headline" aria-live="polite"></div>
          <p class="aipf-onepct" id="aipf-onepct"></p>
          <p class="aipf-note" id="aipf-grid-note"></p>
          <p class="aipf-note">Excludes model training, image and video generation, and retries.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- FULL WIDTH: daily comparison -->
    <section class="aipf-graph" aria-label="Your daily AI use vs everyday things">
      <div class="aipf-graph-head">
        <h2 class="aipf-graph-title">Your daily AI use, next to everyday things</h2>
        <p class="aipf-graph-sub" id="aipf-daily-sub"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="aipf-bars" id="aipf-daily-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </section>

    <!-- FULL WIDTH: words produced & reading time -->
    <section class="aipf-words" aria-label="Words produced">
      <p class="aipf-eyebrow">What you're generating</p>
      <p class="aipf-words-body" id="aipf-words" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </section>

    <!-- FULL WIDTH: annual leverage graph -->
    <section class="aipf-graph" aria-label="A year of your AI use vs bigger choices">
      <div class="aipf-graph-head">
        <h2 class="aipf-graph-title">A year of it, next to the bigger choices in your life</h2>
        <p class="aipf-graph-sub" id="aipf-annual-sub"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="aipf-bars" id="aipf-annual-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </section>

    <!-- Methodology -->
    <details class="aipf-method">
      <summary>Methodology &amp; sources</summary>
      <div class="aipf-method-body">
        <p>
          <strong>Per-model AI estimates.</strong> Energy, carbon, and water per prompt are
          computed with the open-source <a href="https://ecologits.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>
          library (v0.10), the engine behind the
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits calculator</a>;
          the model list is current as of June 2026. EcoLogits estimates electricity from each
          model's (active) parameter count and output length, and adds embodied impacts from
          manufacturing the hardware. The low–high range shown reflects EcoLogits' own 95%
          confidence interval (mostly uncertainty in the parameter counts of closed models). Water
          is EcoLogits' water-consumption footprint (data-center cooling plus the water used to
          generate the electricity).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Carbon on your grid.</strong> EcoLogits reports carbon on the global average grid
          (~0.59 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e/kWh). This page rescales the electricity share to the grid where
          you live — roughly 0.38 (US), 0.25 (EU), 0.20 (UK), 0.54 (China), 0.63 (India)
          kg CO<sub>2</sub>e/kWh — so the AI figure reflects your region. Water is left on the
          global basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Output length.</strong> Each row's length maps to an EcoLogits output-token
          count: a tweet (50 tokens), short email (170), article summary (250), chatbot reply
          (400), 5-page report (5,000), long document (15,000), coding/agent session (100,000),
          and a full novel (500,000). Cost scales with output length, so long generations and agent
          runs dominate. Counts are per day; annual figures multiply by 365. Word counts assume
          ~0.75 words per token; reading time uses 238 words/minute (Brysbaert 2019).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Your footprint.</strong> <em>Where you live</em> is an average per-person
          footprint for home energy, transport, and goods, excluding food and flights to avoid
          double-counting (Our World in Data). <em>Diet</em> uses annual food footprints from Poore
          &amp; Nemecek (2018) and Scarborough et al. (2023): ~1.05 t CO<sub>2</sub>e/yr for a vegan
          diet up to 3.2 t for a heavy-meat diet. <em>Flying</em> uses ~0.255 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e per
          passenger-mile including high-altitude effects. The water footprint includes the
          "virtual" water embedded in food, which dominates it — note this is not the same kind of
          water as the AI figure (mostly data-center cooling), so the comparison shows scale, not
          identical accounting.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Everyday things &amp; lifestyle changes.</strong> A cup of coffee ≈ 0.21 kg
          CO<sub>2</sub>e / 35 gal; an hour on a PS5 ≈ 0.08 kg (~200 W of gameplay); a mile in a gas
          car ≈ 0.40 kg; a 10-minute hot shower ≈ 0.5 kg / 25 gal; printing a 400-page book ≈ 2.5 kg
          / 300 gal; a beef burger ≈ 3 kg / 460 gal. A cotton-heavy pair of jeans ≈ 33 kg / 2,100
          gal; a new smartphone ≈ 70 kg / 3,190 gal; switching from an average to a vegan diet saves
          ≈ 1.45 t/yr and ≈ 237,000 gal/yr; one transatlantic flight ≈ 1.76 t; a year of average US
          driving (~12,000 mi) ≈ 4.8 t (Water Footprint Network, Our World in Data, manufacturer
          LCAs).
        </p>
        <p class="aipf-method-caveat">
          The point of this tool is scale. For almost everyone, day-to-day AI use is a rounding
          error next to diet, driving, home energy, and especially flying. That doesn't mean AI's
          total energy demand is small — but your individual prompts are.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </article>
</Base>

